P0 = [
"Colorado County Boot Camp, later called the Colorado County Juvenile Facility, was a secure facility for juvenile offenders in Eagle Lake, Texas. Correctional Services Corporation (CSC), a private prison company, ran it from 1998.{k98,k04} It held youth from several Texas counties and the state, and grew from 100 to 156 beds by 2002.{k98,k02} On August 31, 2002, a youth died there in what authorities called a suicide.{pln} In 2004 CSC wrote down the facility's value by $1.5 million.{k04}",
"CSC opened the boot camp in 1998 as a 100-bed county detention facility it managed for Colorado County.{k98} In 1999 CSC acquired Youth Services International (YSI), and its juvenile division, including Eagle Lake, operated under the YSI name from then on.{k02,k03} By 2002 the camp had 156 beds and held youth under a contract with several counties and the state. CSC owned part of the site, an expansion it had built, and managed the rest.{k02,k03}",
"On August 31, 2002, a youth died at the boot camp. Authorities called it a \"clear case of suicide\" and did not release the youth's name. Prison Legal News counted the death among many at boot camps nationwide, including others run by YSI.{pln} In the fourth quarter of 2004 CSC found the value of the facility's assets was not recoverable and wrote it down by $1.5 million.{k04} In March 2005 CSC still listed it as a 156-bed secure residential facility under a state and county contract, which it now only managed.{k04} The GEO Group bought CSC later in 2005 and later sold YSI back to its principals.{wpcsc} The facility has since closed; when is not on record.",
]
json.dump({"sources": S0, "paragraphs": P0}, open("rec00.json", "w"), indent=1)

# rec15 Straight - Hampton Roads
